wifi: mt76: mt7615: end rate sets sent at 160 MHz with a robust rate

From the slot in CHANGE_BW_RATE on, the hardware sends a rate set at
another bandwidth. After a set at 160 MHz, those tries went out as
6 Mbit/s OFDM at 20 MHz and were practically never acknowledged in tests
between two MT7615 at -65 to -69 dBm, with the primary channel at the
lowest and at the second 20 MHz channel, although the peer received the
6 Mbit/s beacons fine. With CHANGE_BW_RATE at slot 6, a frame that the
first slots did not get through was lost.

Those first slots are weak at range too: the set is widened to the
bandwidth of its probe or first rate, which turns minstrel's most
probable rate, often a 20 or 40 MHz rate at range, into one that needs up
to 9 dB more signal (3 dB per doubling of the bandwidth, IEEE
802.11ac-2013 22.3.19.1; MCS 6 needs -65 dBm at 20 MHz and -56 dBm at
160 MHz). In those tests 21 of 29 (AP) and 47 of 50 (STA) frames sent
with a set at 160 MHz were lost, and none of 376 sent with a narrower set.

So for a set sent at 160 MHz, move CHANGE_BW_RATE to the last slot and
end the set with the most robust rate at 160 MHz, one stream MCS 0
(-73 dBm): always as the last rate, and as the third rate too if
minstrel's most probable rate is narrower, as how that one does at
160 MHz is unknown. Sets sent at other bandwidths do not change.

--- a/mt7615/mac.c
+++ b/mt7615/mac.c
@@ -1068,7 +1068,7 @@ mt7615_mac_update_rate_desc(struct mt761
 	struct mt7615_dev *dev = phy->dev;
 	struct mt76_phy *mphy = phy->mt76;
 	struct ieee80211_tx_rate *ref;
-	bool rateset, stbc = false, same_bw = false;
+	bool rateset, stbc = false, same_bw = false, robust_tail;
 	int n_rates = sta->n_rates;
 	u8 bw, bw_prev;
 	u32 bw_flags;
@@ -1107,6 +1107,27 @@ mt7615_mac_update_rate_desc(struct mt761
 		if (rates[i].flags & IEEE80211_TX_RC_160_MHZ_WIDTH)
 			same_bw = true;
 
+	/*
+	 * Tries from slot CHANGE_BW_RATE on go out at another bandwidth: from
+	 * a set at 160 MHz as 6 Mbit/s OFDM at 20 MHz, which tests between two
+	 * MT7615 never saw acknowledged. So a set sent at 160 MHz gets
+	 * CHANGE_BW_RATE at the last slot instead and ends with the most
+	 * robust rate there, one stream MCS 0. That also replaces the most
+	 * probable rate if it is narrower, as its success at 160 MHz is unknown.
+	 */
+	robust_tail = bw_flags & IEEE80211_TX_RC_160_MHZ_WIDTH;
+	if (robust_tail) {
+		struct ieee80211_tx_rate tail = {
+			.count = rates[3].count,
+			.flags = IEEE80211_TX_RC_VHT_MCS | bw_flags,
+		};
+
+		ieee80211_rate_set_vht(&tail, 0, 1);
+		if (!(rates[2].flags & IEEE80211_TX_RC_160_MHZ_WIDTH))
+			rates[2] = tail;
+		rates[3] = tail;
+	}
+
 	if (same_bw && probe_rate)
 		mt7615_mac_rate_set_bw(ref, bw_flags);
 
@@ -1175,6 +1196,10 @@ mt7615_mac_update_rate_desc(struct mt761
 	if (bw_prev)
 		rd->bw_idx = 7;
 
+	/* CHANGE_BW_RATE 7, see above */
+	if (robust_tail)
+		rd->bw_idx = 0;
+
 	rd->rateset = rateset;
 	rd->bw = same_bw ? mt7615_mac_rate_bw(bw_flags) : bw;
 
